Phase 3③ — read-side tenant boundary (viewer visibility)
========================================================
Date : 2026-07-28
Gate : make check -> 1377 passed, 1 skipped (1367 -> 1377, +10)
Build: next build OK · tsc clean


What was actually exposed
--------------------------
Phase 3① made the credential the boundary on the **write** side. The read side
had no boundary at all:

    $ curl http://<dashboard>/api/dashboard/platform/status     # no auth
    -> every tenant's add-on status, isolation posture, namespace list,
       quota usage, and the roster of declared-but-silent tenants

On a single-tenant dashboard that is a demo convenience. On a fleet view it is
one tenant reading another's topology, to anyone who asks.

Two policies were written down and neither was the one that ran:

  - `lib/auth.ts` exported `ROUTE_PROTECTION` marking reads `"public"` and
    `meetsProtectionLevel()` to evaluate it. **Nothing imported either.**
    Confirmed by grep across src/ — zero consumers.
  - `middleware.ts`'s matcher listed only write routes, and that is what
    actually executed.

A test was holding the dead code in place:

    tests/test_dashboard_auth_and_activity.py
      assert "export function meetsProtectionLevel" in content
      assert "export const ROUTE_PROTECTION" in content

It asserted the *declaration*, which is how declared-but-unconsumed policy
survives review. Replaced with an assertion about *consumption*.


Where authorization lives now
------------------------------
`dashboard/src/lib/visibility.ts`, called by the route handler next to the data.

This follows Next's own guidance, which is also the rule this repo already paid
for on the Python side (`guard_scoped_action`): one implementation every caller
invokes. From node_modules/next/dist/docs/.../guides/authentication.md:

    "While Proxy can be useful for initial checks, it should not be your only
     line of defense in protecting your data. The majority of security checks
     should be performed as close as possible to your data source"

Three answers, kept distinct — an empty fleet and a fleet you may not see call
for opposite reactions from whoever is looking:

    no session         -> restricted, reason=unauthenticated
    session, no grants -> restricted, reason=no-grants
    admin              -> tenants=null (unfiltered)


Live behaviour (built artifact, `next start`)
----------------------------------------------
    $ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:3199/api/dashboard/platform/status
    {
        "statuses": [], "freshness": [], "tenancy": {}, "missing": [],
        "staleAfterSec": 0, "connected": false, "restricted": true
    }

The hub is not even contacted when the caller is restricted — an unauthenticated
request must not be able to make the dashboard fan out.


The tests execute the policy rather than grep it
-------------------------------------------------
`tests/test_dashboard_visibility.py` compiles `visibility.ts` and runs it under
node. Content assertions cannot catch an inverted condition: every string they
look for stays exactly where it is while the fleet view leaks.

Falsified: injecting the classic fail-open (no grants recorded -> do not filter)
made 2 tests fail:

    FAILED TestDefaultIsNothing::test_signed_in_user_without_grants_sees_nothing
    FAILED TestGrants::test_empty_strings_are_not_grants

Restored -> 8 passed.

`missing` is filtered too. It names declared tenant/envs the hub has never heard
from — so it leaks the tenant roster precisely when those tenants have no status
to leak, which would have made the one field that reveals every tenant name the
one field nobody thought to filter.


Deprecation found while reading the docs first
-----------------------------------------------
`dashboard/AGENTS.md` says to read `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before writing
dashboard code. Doing so surfaced that Next 16 **deprecated the `middleware`
file convention** in favour of `proxy` (v16.0.0). Migrated `src/middleware.ts`
-> `src/proxy.ts` (`export { auth as proxy }`). `next build` now reports
`ƒ Proxy (Middleware)`. The failure mode of leaving it would have been the
write-route matcher silently not running.


WHAT IS NOT PROVEN HERE — read before quoting it
--------------------------------------------------
- **The granted-viewer path was not exercised end-to-end through the route.**
  That needs a real OAuth session. What is proven live is the *restricted* path;
  the filtering itself is proven by executing the module, not by a browser
  round-trip. A live browser pass with a granted viewer is still owed.

- **Only the fleet endpoint is partitioned.** `/api/dashboard/incidents`,
  `/deployments`, `/activities` remain unauthenticated and unpartitioned. They
  are not tenant-labelled in the read model yet, so filtering them is a data-model
  change, not a guard. Until then, do not describe the dashboard as
  tenant-isolated — only the fleet view is.

- **Grants are stored, not verified against the registry.** A grant naming a
  tenant that does not exist is accepted and simply matches nothing.
